module gunbc.rung_drop

import std.types { String, NonEmptyStr, List, Bool }
import gunbc.guarantee_rung {
  GuaranteeRung, OutsideTheLadder, Mitigatable, MechanicallyPreventable,
  StructurallyGuaranteed, StructurallyImpossible, guarantee_rung_label,
}

// THIS MODULE IS THE SINGLE AUTHORITY FOR A DESIGN section 4b(3) DECLARED DROP -- THE RECORD AND
// THE POPULATION BOTH. It was not, and the fork was live in main: gunbc.guarantee_rung_drop carried
// a SECOND record for the same declaration, `GuaranteeRungDrop`, with the five 4b(3) fields typed,
// and four Standing drops were declared through it IN SITU at their own subjects' modules. Those
// four appeared in no roster, in the projected drop roster nowhere, and in DESIGN.md's "the ones
// standing today" list nowhere -- while DESIGN section 4b(3) states that declared drops are rostered
// IN FULL and names this module as the authority. That is not untidiness. It is the document that
// states the obligation under-reporting the live safety accounting by four rows, in a file loaded in
// full on every turn of every session. The fork is removed by cutting at the root: GuaranteeRungDrop
// is deleted, not deprecated, and its rows, its refusal rendering and its witness land here.
//
// CUTTING A MODULE AT THE ROOT INVALIDATES ITS CITATIONS, AND THE SWEEP THAT FINDS THEM NEEDS A
// DISCRIMINATOR THAT IS NOT BACKTICKS AND NOT TENSE. Deleting gunbc.guarantee_rung_drop left eight
// textual occurrences of the name across three authorities and their three projections. Two were
// LIVE and were repaired here and in gunbc.recurring_failure_mode; the rest stand. The rule that
// separates them is whether the sentence ASSERTS SOMETHING TRUE OF THE PRESENT CORPUS or RECOUNTS
// AN EVENT. An assertion ("carries", "is filed as") becomes FALSE when the module is deleted; a
// recounting ("carried", "the deleted", "gunbc#10021 appended one row to it") becomes MORE true,
// because the deletion is what makes the past tense correct.
//
// TWO PROXIES FAIL ON THIS POPULATION AND BOTH WERE TRIED. Backticked-means-live and past-tense-
// means-historical each classify four of the five authority occurrences correctly and DISAGREE on
// the fifth, which is backticked AND surrounded by past-tense verbs, so a rule built on either
// returns no answer for it rather than a wrong one. That occurrence is also a third form neither
// proxy names: a backticked FILE PATH, `dag/gunbc/guarantee_rung_drop.dag`, recounting a merge
// conflict that happened in a file that existed at the time. Under the assertion-versus-event rule
// it stands, and it is recorded as a DECISION rather than as a row a narrow pattern failed to
// reach -- an assert-count-is-1 guard that passes because the pattern is narrow reads exactly like
// one that passes because the file is clean.
//
// THE OTHER HALF OF THAT MODULE WAS A DIFFERENT SUBJECT AND SURVIVES UNDER ITS OWN NAME. A
// section 4b(2) STALL -- a class sitting below its attainable ceiling, naming what blocks the climb
// -- is not a variant of a drop: nothing FELL when a stall begins, so previous/temporary/reason have
// no answer and forcing one would fabricate an event. Those rows are gunbc.guarantee_stall now, and
// the ladder both subjects are stated in is gunbc.guarantee_rung, imported by both rather than
// homed inside either.

// ONE ROW PER DECLARED RUNG DROP, FOR THE REASON gunbc.recurring_failure_mode ALREADY RECORDS ONE
// ROW PER FAILURE CLASS. These five declarations lived as five `li(text:)` blobs inside
// gunbc.design_document's Building & checks list -- 64,977 characters, 40% of the whole projected
// DESIGN.md, in a section whose other three items are two shell commands and an actuator
// invocation. They are not build instructions. They are DESIGN section 4b(3) declarations, and
// 4b(3) names their fields for them: previous rung, temporary rung, reason, bounded population,
// restoration trigger. A declaration whose shape the design document specifies is a record, and
// storing it as one anonymous String inside a markdown builder is the section 4c defect -- a
// machine-consumed fact held as commentary -- committed in the authority that states the rule.
//
// THIS CUT IS MECHANICAL AND ITS ORACLE IS CONTENT PRESERVATION. Each row carries its authored
// paragraph BYTE-FOR-BYTE in `authored`; only the typed `identity`, `subject` and `declared` fields
// are new, and they are read off the paragraph own opening sentence rather than composed. The
// projection renders the roster in source order into the file `gunbc.design_ledgers` names, so every
// sentence that
// was in DESIGN.md is still generated from this repository and still adjudicated by the
// generated-artifact phase -- what changed is WHICH file it lands in, and therefore what every
// session loads on every turn.
//
// ONE ROW PER FILE, AND THE REASON IS gunbc.recurring_failure_mode `merge_region_excludes_shared_tail`,
// WHICH THIS CARRIER IS THE WORKED SPECIMEN OF IN BOTH DIRECTIONS. The rows were one file of
// multi-line blocks, whose shared closing brace made two-lane appends resolve into a row missing its
// tail; they were then one file of one-line rows, which removed that suffix and made every row a
// multi-kilobyte line, so a lane flipping `standing` and a lane extending `authored` on the SAME row
// collided with certainty -- three lanes in one night. That row carries the corrected rule and the
// reason both shapes fail; it is not restated here. What is carrier-specific is the answer: a row is
// a FILE under `dag/gunbc/rung_drop/`, so two lanes amending two rows share no line and a lane
// declaring a NEW drop adds a file git resolves with no merge at all. What text remains shared is
// enumerations only, `gunbc.rung_drop.roster` first among them at one bare identifier per row: two
// lanes appending there still conflict -- measured, two regions -- but each region is a whole line on
// both sides with no shared suffix, so the additive resolution is correct and total, which is the one
// property the one-line row bought and this shape keeps. The next paragraph counts the rest of them,
// because "the only text left shared" is the sentence a reader would cite as though it were four
// fewer lists than it is.
//
// THE SHARED APPEND POINTS THAT REMAIN, COUNTED RATHER THAN CLAIMED AWAY, because a split that
// reports "no shared text" while three shared lists survive is the rung inflation this carrier is
// about. A NEW PROSE drop touches four places: its own new file (no merge), one roster line in
// `gunbc.rung_drop.roster`, one arm in `LegacyProseIdentity`, and one line in
// `legacy_prose_identity_roster` -- plus one arm in `legacy_prose_identity`, which is a match block
// and therefore a fifth. A NEW TYPED drop touches two: its file and the roster line. So the split
// removes the row bodies from the conflict surface and leaves the ENUMERATIONS on it, and what makes
// those tolerable is not that they are absent but that every one of them is a WHOLE LINE with no
// shared suffix -- the additive resolution is correct at each. That is a strictly weaker claim than
// "disjoint edits no longer conflict", and it is the true one: two lanes declaring two new prose
// drops still conflict in four regions. They just resolve by keeping both sides.
//
// AND THE TYPED ARM IS THE ONE WITH NO SHARED APPEND POINT AT ALL, which is worth saying because it
// is the direction the debt is supposed to move. `RungDropDeclaration`'s prose arm is enumerated on
// purpose -- the note below records why -- so the four lists above are the visible cost of the arm,
// and they go away with it rather than needing their own repair. A per-row split that fills the five
// 4b(3) fields deletes one arm from each of the three lists as it lands.
//
// THE BLANK LINE BETWEEN FIELDS IS LOAD-BEARING AND IS NOT FORMATTING. One field per line does NOT
// make two field edits independent: a three-way merge needs an UNCHANGED line BETWEEN two changed
// regions to keep them apart, so a `standing` flip and an `authored` extension conflict when those
// fields are adjacent, exactly as they did on one line. Measured with `git merge-file` on
// `floor_cut_heal`: one-line row, 1 conflict; one field per line, adjacent, 1 conflict; separated by
// a blank line, 0 conflicts with both edits present. Removing the blank lines here restores the
// defect this carrier was reshaped to remove.
//
// THE RUNG IS UNCHANGED AND IS STATED RATHER THAN CLAIMED BY THE SPLIT, AND THIS PARAGRAPH IS NOT A
// WALL. Every property above is enforced by nothing. A second `data` row added to one of these files
// reintroduces the geometry; deleting the blank lines reintroduces the field-adjacency conflict; and
// neither is refused by any executing check, so the reader who does the damage is precisely the
// reader not reading this. A note that says "load-bearing" informs whoever is already looking, which
// is an inert lens and is why the rung stays where it is. RUNG: 1, mitigated -- the shape is safe by
// default and by nothing else. CEILING: 2, mechanically preventable; not higher, because git's text
// merge is external reality and no model here can make a shared line unwritable in another clone.
// NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, NAMING THE CAPABILITY AND NOT AN ARTIFACT: a required-lane consumer that
// EXECUTES a witness whose inputs are recorded from a non-hermetic origin, sufficient for a
// three-arm merge probe to produce a VERDICT rather than a route gap.
//
// WHY THE TRIGGER IS THAT AND NOT "WRITE THE PROBE", established from the tree rather than
// predicted. The probe itself is expressible today: `extdeps.git.plumbing` already models git
// plumbing with typed argv, existing wet witnesses already reach `extdeps.shell` and
// `extdeps.filesystem.filesystem_io`, and the three arms above ARE the probe. What it would not do
// is produce a verdict. Its nearest existing analogue is `test.claim.fleet_revision_relation_wet_matrix`,
// a witness matrix over real git operations: `v2.workflow.floor_route_gap` declares all seven of its
// identities as expected route gaps, every one `operation: "MergeBase"` with ground `NoMockResponse`,
// and the recorded fixtures for that operation EXIST in the corpus while the matrix still yields no
// verdict. The contrast is inside the same subject: `test.claim.fleet_main_revision_witness_test`
// consumes the same fleet-revision concept, is in no gap row, and executes ordinary fns. So the line
// the required lane draws is not hermetic-versus-wet posture but INPUT ORIGIN -- a witness that
// constructs its inputs executes, a matrix over recorded external operations route-gaps -- and
// `Record` mode does not cross it, since the fixtures being present is exactly the case measured.
//
// SO THE PROBE IS DELIBERATELY NOT WRITTEN HERE. Landing it would add rows to the route-gap roster
// and buy no wall, and a probe cited in a change as the evidence for its own fix while producing no
// verdict is the rung inflation this ladder exists to refuse. The separation therefore rests on this
// paragraph, which is prose, and that is the honest state until the trigger above fires.
//
// WHICH SIDE OF THE PROJECTION LINE THESE FILES SIT ON, because the answer decides whether the
// separation can survive. They are HAND-AUTHORED. `gunbc.generated_artifact` emits exactly three
// `.dag` artifacts, all under `dag/gunbc/stage0/`, and no registry row names anything here; the one
// generated artifact in this family is `docs/design-rung-drops.md`, which these rows feed. So today
// nothing regenerates a row file and the blank lines survive because no producer touches them. If a
// producer is ever given these files, EMITTING THE SEPARATION IS PART OF ITS CONTRACT: an emitter
// that renders a row without it silently restores the defect on the first projection, and would do
// so while every content oracle stayed green, since the projection's bytes do not depend on it.
//
// WHY 21 ROWS STILL CARRY THEIR DECLARATION AS PROSE, AND WHY THAT IS A CLOSED SET RATHER THAN AN
// OPTION. Splitting `authored` into the five 4b(3) fields means deciding, per row, which of its
// sentences is the reason and which is the population -- and several of these paragraphs argue at
// length about exactly that question, one of them declaring that its population is unbounded and
// that this is an unmet requirement rather than a satisfied one. Deriving typed fields from that is
// semantic rewriting with no oracle: it is fabricated plausible output, and it would be
// unfalsifiable afterwards because nothing retains the original. So the record states the
// difference instead of hiding it -- `RungDropDeclaration` has a typed arm and a prose arm, and
// which arm a row is in is a FOLD over one roster rather than an invisible condition.
//
// THE PROSE ARM IS ENUMERATED, WHICH IS THE WHOLE REASON IT IS ADMISSIBLE. An arm that any future
// row could choose without saying so is the cheapest way to declare a drop, so the typed fields
// would never get filled and the coproduct would stop being a debt record and become the default --
// the absorbing fallback with a nicer type name. `LegacyProseIdentity` therefore names every prose
// row individually, and a row cannot enter the arm without an edit to this enumeration that a
// reader sees in the diff. Each per-row split deletes one arm, and the arm count is the remaining
// debt.
//
// THE RUNG IS MECHANICALLY PREVENTABLE, NOT STRUCTURALLY IMPOSSIBLE, AND THE CORRECTION IS RECORDED
// HERE BECAUSE THIS CARRIER FIRST CLAIMED OTHERWISE. The first version of this note said a further
// prose row had NO CONSTRUCTOR and was structurally impossible. That was falsified within hours of
// being authored, and by ordinary work rather than by an attack: `spark_serving_local_artifact_not_reproducible`
// was authored on another lane against the pre-consolidation shape and landed in main while the
// consolidation was still open. Admitting it cost exactly one arm. So what the enumeration
// guarantees is that the arm cannot grow SILENTLY -- growth is an edit to a closed list, visible in
// review and counted by the fold below -- and what it does not guarantee is that the arm cannot
// grow. Leaving the stronger sentence standing would have been the rung inflation DESIGN
// section 4b(1) names, in the carrier whose whole subject is honest rung accounting.
//
// WHY THAT ROW WAS ADMITTED AS PROSE RATHER THAN TYPED, since the alternative was available and was
// rejected on the same ground the other rows are: its paragraph states its previous rung, its
// temporary rung and its restoration trigger verbatim, but NOT its population and NOT its reason.
// Filling those two by reading the prose is the semantic rewriting with no oracle that this arm
// exists to avoid, and doing it to a row authored on another lane would be doing it to someone
// else's declaration.

// WHETHER THE ROW'S RESTORATION TRIGGER HAS FIRED, MODELED RATHER THAN LEFT TO ITS PARAGRAPH.
// DESIGN 4b(3) says a drop is retired BY ITS TRIGGER AND BY NOTHING ELSE, so retirement is a
// fact each row owns about itself. It had no field, so the only place it was written was prose
// inside `authored` -- and gunbc.design_document projects the "The ones standing today" list as
// an UNFILTERED map over the whole roster, which cannot read a paragraph. The result was that
// DESIGN.md asserted a retired drop as currently standing, in a document loaded in full on every
// turn of every session; two sessions independently built work on that false premise before it
// was caught. A predicate over the existing data could not have fixed it: there was no data.
//
// THE FIELD IS AUTHORED AND NOTHING DERIVES IT FROM THE PARAGRAPH, which is a second
// representation of one fact and is stated here rather than smuggled. `authored` is carried
// byte-for-byte on purpose -- this module's own note above records why splitting it into typed
// fields has no oracle and is not attempted -- so the honest available move is a typed field
// beside the prose, the same one gunbc.deleted_cadence_reference_census made when it carried
// `lost_with` beside a drop rather than widening the shared carrier. The residual risk is
// therefore real and named: a row whose paragraph announces retirement while its field still
// says Standing would drift exactly as the list did. What this change removes is the SECOND
// authority for the standing list, not the gap between a paragraph and its field.
type RungDropStanding
  = Standing
  | Retired { trigger_fired: NonEmptyStr }

// THE REASON A RUNG FELL, A CLOSED COPRODUCT BECAUSE THE REASONS ARE A SMALL NAMED SET AND NAMING
// THEM IS WHAT LETS THE SAME CLASS BE RECOGNISED TWICE.
// LostAsPassenger is the class this carrier was first written for: nothing deleted the subject,
// something the subject rode on was deleted. A deletion census accounts for what it deleted and
// never for what was riding on it, so this reason is invisible to the cut that causes it and the
// carrier it names is the thing whose own re-add will NOT restore the passenger.
type RungDropReason
  = LostAsPassenger { carrier: String }
  | DeletedWithoutReplacement
  | ReplacementStaged { replacement: String }

// THE ENUMERATED SET OF ROWS WHOSE DECLARATION IS STILL PROSE. One nullary arm per such row -- see
// the note above on what that enumeration does and does not guarantee, and on the concurrent row
// that established the difference. The spelling of each identity lives in
// `legacy_prose_identity` and nowhere else, so the row's `identity` field and its arm are joined by
// one authority; test.claim.rung_drop_standing_partition_witness_test asserts that join as an exact
// set equality in both directions, which is what would go red if a row and its arm disagreed.
type LegacyProseIdentity
  = FloorCut
  | MeasurementBankruptcy
  | RegenProducer
  | CitedSymbolCensus
  | EmitStageBlocking
  | LensEnforcementCensuses
  | RequiredGateBankruptcy
  | TextBoundaryIdentityWall
  | FabricEvidenceGating
  | EmittedBytesWitnessRequiredLane
  | DirectCallArgSeamV2Exemption
  | FloorCostClaimQualificationUnavailable
  | SparkRoleScopedRetirementProductionRoot
  | FloorCutHeal
  | FloorCutEffectGates
  | FloorCutFmtGate
  | FloorCutMergeAdmissionStamping
  | FloorCutFalsifierCadence
  | BuiltinSignatureArityPairingFabricates
  | ConcatBinarySignatureExemptFromArgBinding
  | NamespaceAdmissionConsumedRowDeletion
  | SparkServingLocalArtifactNotReproducible
  | DashboardMergeReadySemanticAdmissibility
  | FloorCutRegenSecondGenerationAgreement
  | FloorCutReceiptDiscriminatingArms
  | FloorCutBehaviouralRegressionDifferential
  | RustUnitTestsOffTheMergePath
  | EmitCopyQualificationWithoutAConsumer

fn legacy_prose_identity(l: LegacyProseIdentity) -> String {
  match l {
    FloorCut => "floor_cut"
    MeasurementBankruptcy => "measurement_bankruptcy"
    RegenProducer => "regen_producer"
    CitedSymbolCensus => "cited_symbol_census"
    EmitStageBlocking => "emit_stage_blocking"
    LensEnforcementCensuses => "lens_enforcement_censuses"
    RequiredGateBankruptcy => "required_gate_bankruptcy"
    TextBoundaryIdentityWall => "text_boundary_identity_wall"
    FabricEvidenceGating => "fabric_evidence_gating"
    RustUnitTestsOffTheMergePath => "rust_unit_tests_off_the_merge_path"
    EmitCopyQualificationWithoutAConsumer => "emit_copy_qualification_without_a_consumer"
    EmittedBytesWitnessRequiredLane => "emitted_bytes_witness_required_lane"
    DirectCallArgSeamV2Exemption => "direct_call_arg_seam_v2_exemption"
    FloorCostClaimQualificationUnavailable => "floor_cost_claim_qualification_unavailable"
    SparkRoleScopedRetirementProductionRoot => "spark_role_scoped_retirement_production_root"
    FloorCutHeal => "floor_cut_heal"
    FloorCutEffectGates => "floor_cut_effect_gates"
    FloorCutFmtGate => "floor_cut_fmt_gate"
    FloorCutMergeAdmissionStamping => "floor_cut_merge_admission_stamping"
    FloorCutFalsifierCadence => "floor_cut_falsifier_cadence"
    BuiltinSignatureArityPairingFabricates => "builtin_signature_arity_pairing_fabricates"
    ConcatBinarySignatureExemptFromArgBinding => "concat_binary_signature_exempt_from_arg_binding"
    NamespaceAdmissionConsumedRowDeletion => "namespace_admission_consumed_row_deletion"
    SparkServingLocalArtifactNotReproducible => "spark_serving_local_artifact_not_reproducible"
    DashboardMergeReadySemanticAdmissibility => "dashboard_merge_ready_semantic_admissibility"
    FloorCutRegenSecondGenerationAgreement => "floor_cut_regen_second_generation_agreement"
    FloorCutReceiptDiscriminatingArms => "floor_cut_receipt_discriminating_arms"
    FloorCutBehaviouralRegressionDifferential => "floor_cut_behavioural_regression_differential"
  }
}

// THE DECLARATION ITSELF: SECTION 4b(3)'s FIVE FIELDS, OR THE PARAGRAPH THAT PREDATES THEM.
// `TypedDeclaration` is the shape 4b(3) specifies -- previous rung, temporary rung, reason, bounded
// population, restoration trigger -- and it is the arm every new drop is declared through.
type RungDropDeclaration
  = TypedDeclaration {
      previous: GuaranteeRung
      temporary: GuaranteeRung
      reason: RungDropReason
      population: List<String>
      restoration_trigger: String
    }
  | AuthoredProse { legacy: LegacyProseIdentity, authored: String }

type RungDrop {
  identity: NonEmptyStr
  subject: String
  declared: String
  standing: RungDropStanding
  declaration: RungDropDeclaration
}

// THE ONE RENDERING, so a refusal arm never restates the row beside it. A gate that spelled its
// own message would be a second authority for the same fact (DESIGN section 3), and the two would
// drift in the ordinary way: someone updates the row and the refusal keeps telling the reader
// something that stopped being true. The prose arm renders as its own paragraph, which IS its
// declaration text -- not a fabricated reconstruction of fields it does not have.
fn rung_drop_declaration_message(d: RungDrop) -> String {
  match d.declaration {
    TypedDeclaration {
      previous: p, temporary: t, reason: rs, population: pop, restoration_trigger: rt
    } => concat(
      concat(d.subject, concat(": RUNG DROP, ", guarantee_rung_label(r: p))),
      concat(
        concat(" -> ", concat(guarantee_rung_label(r: t), concat(" (", rung_drop_reason_label(r: rs)))),
        concat(
          concat("). Population: ", join(pop, ", ")),
          concat(". Restored when: ", concat(rt, "."))
        )
      )
    )
    AuthoredProse { legacy: _, authored: a } => a
  }
}

fn rung_drop_reason_label(r: RungDropReason) -> String {
  match r {
    LostAsPassenger { carrier: c } => concat("lost as a passenger of ", c)
    DeletedWithoutReplacement => "deleted without replacement"
    ReplacementStaged { replacement: r2 } => concat("replacement staged: ", r2)
  }
}

// WHETHER THE ROW SITS OFF THE LADDER, AS A THREE-WAY JUDGMENT RATHER THAN A Bool. A prose row does
// not carry a temporary rung, so there is no honest true or false for it -- answering either would
// be the fabricated plausible output DESIGN section 5 forbids, and answering `false` in particular
// would silently report an off-ladder class as on it. NotJudgeableFromProse is the refusal arm, and
// it is what gives the prose arm a visible cost at every consumer rather than a free ride.
type OffLadderJudgment
  = OffLadder
  | OnLadderAt { rung: GuaranteeRung }
  | NotJudgeableFromProse

fn rung_drop_off_ladder_judgment(d: RungDrop) -> OffLadderJudgment {
  match d.declaration {
    TypedDeclaration {
      previous: _, temporary: t, reason: _, population: _, restoration_trigger: _
    } => match t {
      OutsideTheLadder => OffLadder
      Mitigatable => OnLadderAt { rung: t }
      MechanicallyPreventable => OnLadderAt { rung: t }
      StructurallyGuaranteed => OnLadderAt { rung: t }
      StructurallyImpossible => OnLadderAt { rung: t }
    }
    AuthoredProse { legacy: _, authored: _ } => NotJudgeableFromProse
  }
}

// THE REMAINING PROSE DEBT, AS A FOLD OVER THE ONE ROSTER. This is the property the coproduct buys:
// how many declarations are still unsplit is a number this repository can read, rather than a
// condition nobody can see. It reaches zero when every row carries TypedDeclaration, at which point
// the prose arm and LegacyProseIdentity are both deleted -- the dissolution is the arm going away,
// not a flag being flipped.
fn authored_prose_rung_drops(ds: List<RungDrop>) -> List<RungDrop> {
  filter(ds, d => match d.declaration {
    TypedDeclaration {
      previous: _, temporary: _, reason: _, population: _, restoration_trigger: _
    } => false
    AuthoredProse { legacy: _, authored: _ } => true
  })
}

// THE IDENTITIES THE PROSE ARM CLAIMS, READ OFF THE ARM RATHER THAN OFF THE ROW. Paired with the
// identities the rows themselves carry, this is the join that would go red if a row and its arm
// disagreed -- the assertion lives in test.claim.rung_drop_standing_partition_witness_test, in both
// directions, because one direction alone admits a row whose arm belongs to a different drop.
fn authored_prose_arm_identities(ds: List<RungDrop>) -> List<String> {
  map(authored_prose_rung_drops(ds), d => match d.declaration {
    TypedDeclaration {
      previous: _, temporary: _, reason: _, population: _, restoration_trigger: _
    } => ""
    AuthoredProse { legacy: l, authored: _ } => legacy_prose_identity(l: l)
  })
}

// THE CLOSED PROSE UNIVERSE, ENUMERATED, so the debt is joined at IDENTITY grain against a
// controlled roster rather than compared to a number somebody typed. A count equality against a
// literal would be the change detector DESIGN section 5 rejects; this list is the subject universe
// itself, and the coproduct above is what makes it closed. An arm deleted by a per-row split must
// leave here too or the join refuses -- which is the typed disposition the debt contract requires.
data legacy_prose_identity_roster: List<LegacyProseIdentity> = [
  FloorCut,
  MeasurementBankruptcy,
  RegenProducer,
  CitedSymbolCensus,
  EmitStageBlocking,
  LensEnforcementCensuses,
  RequiredGateBankruptcy,
  TextBoundaryIdentityWall,
  FabricEvidenceGating,
  RustUnitTestsOffTheMergePath,
  EmitCopyQualificationWithoutAConsumer,
  EmittedBytesWitnessRequiredLane,
  DirectCallArgSeamV2Exemption,
  FloorCostClaimQualificationUnavailable,
  SparkRoleScopedRetirementProductionRoot,
  FloorCutHeal,
  FloorCutEffectGates,
  FloorCutFmtGate,
  FloorCutMergeAdmissionStamping,
  FloorCutFalsifierCadence,
  BuiltinSignatureArityPairingFabricates,
  ConcatBinarySignatureExemptFromArgBinding,
  NamespaceAdmissionConsumedRowDeletion,
  SparkServingLocalArtifactNotReproducible,
  DashboardMergeReadySemanticAdmissibility,
  FloorCutBehaviouralRegressionDifferential,
  FloorCutReceiptDiscriminatingArms,
  FloorCutRegenSecondGenerationAgreement,
]

// EVERY PROSE ROW NAMES ITS OWN IDENTITY. Set equality between the arms and the rows would pass on
// two rows that had swapped arms, so the agreement is asserted PER ROW against the row's own
// identity field. This is the check that gives the two spellings one authority.
fn authored_prose_identity_agrees(d: RungDrop) -> Bool {
  match d.declaration {
    TypedDeclaration {
      previous: _, temporary: _, reason: _, population: _, restoration_trigger: _
    } => true
    AuthoredProse { legacy: l, authored: _ } => legacy_prose_identity(l: l) == d.identity
  }
}

fn every_authored_prose_row_names_its_arm(ds: List<RungDrop>) -> Bool {
  fold(ds, init: true, f: fn(acc, d) { acc && authored_prose_identity_agrees(d: d) })
}

// EVERY ARM IS CLAIMED BY EXACTLY ONE ROW -- the other direction, and the one that catches an arm
// silently going unused (a row deleted rather than split) or claimed twice.
fn every_legacy_arm_is_claimed_exactly_once(ds: List<RungDrop>) -> Bool {
  let claimed = authored_prose_arm_identities(ds)
  fold(legacy_prose_identity_roster, init: true, f: fn(acc, l) {
    acc && length(xs: filter(claimed, i => i == legacy_prose_identity(l: l))) == 1
  })
}

// THE FOUR ROWS THIS CONSOLIDATION RESCUED, ENUMERATED BY NAME, because nothing else pins them.
// The prose side is pinned from both ends -- every arm claimed exactly once, every row agreeing
// with its arm -- so a prose row that vanished would refuse. The typed side had no such anchor: a
// fold over the roster answers the same for four typed rows as for three, and the roster-shape
// count compares the prose arm against the arm roster and never looks at the typed side at all. So
// a consolidated row could have disappeared in a later merge and every assertion in this file would
// still have passed. These four were rostered NOWHERE before this change, which is the whole
// finding, and a control that could lose one silently would be the finding recurring inside its own
// repair.
//
// PRESENCE, NOT SET EQUALITY, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS DELIBERATE. Asserting that the typed rows are
// EXACTLY these four would refuse the next honest typed drop -- the growth this consolidation
// exists to make the normal path -- which is a change detector on healthy work. Asserting that each
// of these four is present exactly once refuses only what must never happen: one of them silently
// leaving. A new row is admitted; a lost row is not.
data consolidated_typed_drop_identities: List<String> = [
  "source_root_ingest_gate",
  "spark_serving_fleet_global_configuration",
  "witness_deferral_freeze_forward_rule",
  "deleted_cadence_reference",
]

fn every_consolidated_typed_drop_is_rostered_once(ds: List<RungDrop>) -> Bool {
  let ids = map(ds, d => d.identity)
  fold(consolidated_typed_drop_identities, init: true, f: fn(acc, want) {
    acc && length(xs: filter(ids, i => i == want)) == 1
  })
}
